---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { tagPath } from '../utils/reading-time';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");

const topics = [...new Set(
  (await getCollection('writing')).flatMap((post) => post.data.tags)
)].sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, 'en'));
---

<Layout title="About" description="From optimization to open-ended discovery: why Global Minima, and an invitation to think and build with Jizhou Xu.">
  <main id="main-content" class="post-container" tabindex="-1">
    <article>
      <div class="about-prose">
        <section aria-labelledby="about-global-minima">
          <h1 id="about-global-minima" class="about-title">Why Global Minima</h1>
          <p>
            A global minimum is the lowest value a function attains across all
            allowed inputs. Picture a landscape: a local minimum is the bottom
            of one valley; a global minimum is the lowest elevation anywhere
            on the map. Several different places can share that elevation.
          </p>
          <p>
            The shape of the landscape changes the search. In a
            <a href="https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/bv_cvxbook.pdf#page=152">convex problem</a>,
            the landscape has a bowl-like geometry: every local minimum is
            global. On rougher terrain, following the downhill slope
            can leave a search in a shallower valley. Trying different starting
            points may uncover a better solution, but finding the best one so
            far and proving that nothing better exists are different achievements.
          </p>
          <p>
            More computation can widen the search, provided the method can
            make use of it. In
            <a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~eunsol/courses/data/bitter_lesson.pdf">The Bitter Lesson</a>,
            Richard Sutton argues that AI methods built around search and
            learning have repeatedly benefited more from growing computational
            power than approaches built around human expertise. His argument
            favors systems that can keep discovering over systems limited to
            what their designers already understand. I see a useful principle
            here for thinking, too: give an idea a way to meet evidence you did
            not anticipate.
          </p>
          <p>
            I use the plural because there are many problems worth working on,
            each with its own measure of better. Outside mathematics, deciding
            what to optimize is itself part of the work. We often settle into
            local minima: an explanation that survives familiar objections,
            a routine that escapes further thought. Another field, experiment,
            or person may show us what we missed. The search may never be
            finished. I enjoy the journey, including the difficult stretches,
            and I have no intention of giving up.
          </p>
          <p>The world might just be a playground simulator, so why not have fun?</p>
        </section>

        <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-author">
          <h2 id="about-author" class="about-section-heading">Jizhou Xu</h2>
          <p>
            Labels are hard, but I think of myself as an ensemble: a builder,
            a researcher, and a backer of ideas. My interests run through
            science, technology, and markets. I like tracing an argument to
            its assumptions, building something that puts it to a test, and
            working with people to give promising ideas a fair chance.
          </p>
          <figure class="about-quote">
            <blockquote>
              <p>What I cannot create, I do not understand.</p>
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption>
              <a href="https://digital.archives.caltech.edu/collections/Images/1.10-67/">Richard Feynman</a>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>
            I write here to put those interests into conversation with yours.
            You might know a field I am only beginning to understand; I might
            see a connection that is easier to notice from outside it. If an
            essay leaves you with a question, an objection, or something you
            want to test, I would like to hear from you. We can follow it
            further together.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="about-topics">
        <h2 id="about-topics" class="about-section-heading">Topics</h2>
        <nav class="about-tags" aria-label="Writing topics">
          {
            topics.map((topic) => (
              <a href={`${base}${tagPath(topic)}`} class="tag">
                {topic}
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </nav>
      </section>

      <section class="about-section">
        <h2 class="about-section-heading">GitHub Activity</h2>
        <div class="github-chart-wrapper">
          <div class="github-chart-frame" role="region" aria-label="GitHub contribution calendar" tabindex="0">
            <img
              class="github-chart-light"
              src="https://ghchart.rshah.org/315C4B/jizhouxu"
              alt="GitHub contribution calendar for jizhouxu"
              width="663"
              height="104"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <img
              class="github-chart-dark"
              src="https://ghchart.rshah.org/474544/jizhouxu"
              alt="GitHub contribution calendar for jizhouxu"
              width="663"
              height="104"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <p class="github-chart-caption">
            <a
              href="https://github.com/jizhouxu"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer">jizhouxu</a
            > · github.com
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
